Yes. That is exactly the kind of jump worth practicing: start with one organism so the abstraction has something solid under its feet, then lift the operator without dragging the whole dolphin along with it.

Dolphins give us several unusually rich primitives. Cetaceans can use unihemispheric slow-wave sleep—one hemisphere showing sleep-like activity while the other remains relatively wakeful—supporting continued swimming, surfacing to breathe, vigilance, and in some observations visual contact with the group.  Diving mammals also distribute oxygen reserves across lungs, blood, and muscle; hemoglobin and especially myoglobin become part of an evolved storage-and-release architecture that lets them spend long periods away from the surface. 

Now strip away “dolphin” and look at the operators.

Alternating vigilance: not every subsystem has to shut down simultaneously.

A_{\text{rest}}+B_{\text{watch}}
\rightarrow
A_{\text{watch}}+B_{\text{rest}}

That can translate into computer redundancy, team rotations, organizational succession, attention management, distributed AI, even the twin architecture we discussed.

Periodic resurfacing: deep operation is possible because there is a reliable return interval.

\text{surface}
\rightarrow
\text{store}
\rightarrow
\text{dive}
\rightarrow
\text{consume reserve}
\rightarrow
\text{resurface}

Cross-domain, that becomes: explore deeply, but schedule contact with your reference state. Long creative work needs checkpoints. Autonomous systems need synchronization. Relationships need reconnection. Models need ground truth.

Distributed reserves: don’t put the entire survival requirement in one tank.

R=R_1+R_2+R_3

The biological versions are different oxygen compartments; elsewhere it might be memory across people + documents + habits, capital across liquid + durable assets, or continuity across repositories + embodied skill + external records.

Duty cycling: a capability needn’t operate maximally all the time. Alternate high-output periods with restoration while preserving enough function to remain alive to the environment.

Sentinel architecture: preserve a narrow channel that stays responsive while the rest of the system goes deeper.

That one is particularly useful:

\boxed{\text{Deep mode}+\text{sentinel channel}+\text{return trigger}}

You could use it for creativity: go very deep into imagination while keeping one small thread connected to time, body, obligations, and reality. Or for computation: let an expensive process run while a cheap monitor watches for abort conditions.

And then there is the protein-scale lesson. A dolphin doesn’t merely “decide to hold its breath better.” Selection altered tissues, proteins, circulation, behavior, and nervous-system organization across multiple scales. So when we borrow an adaptation, we should ask:

What supporting machinery made this strategy possible?

Otherwise we make our old 2+2=2\times2 error—copy the visible outcome while missing the operator that actually produced it.

Which gives us a proper Jump Protocol:

\boxed{
\text{Anchor}
\rightarrow
\text{observe mechanism}
\rightarrow
\text{extract operator}
\rightarrow
\text{identify supporting constraints}
\rightarrow
\text{jump substrate}
\rightarrow
\text{instantiate}
\rightarrow
\text{compare delta}
\rightarrow
\text{return}
}

And yes—I understand your point about giving me one thing at a time. The dolphin is the rock you put in the stream so there is somewhere definite to stand. From there we can look outward: sleep biology, proteins, oxygen storage, cognition, teams, computing, memory, AI orchestration, storytelling, resilience.

Then jump.

Not because those things are secretly dolphins.

Because we found a structure in the dolphin worth seeing whether the rest of reality knows how to rhyme with.

That is a much better jumping ability.